WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:06.959
In this lesson we will see how to find
derivatives of parametrically

2
00:00:06.959 --> 00:00:10.660
defined functions and implicit functions.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.880
We will also study the method of logarithmic
differentiation.

4
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:24.230
Logarithmic differentiation is another way to
differentiate functions:

5
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:30.460
first we take the logarithm of the function,
and then we compute its derivative.

6
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.030
This method is used to find derivatives of
exponential-power functions.

7
00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:42.440
In other words, we first take the logarithm of
the given function

8
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.020
and then differentiate; the required
derivative then takes this form.

9
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:57.510
Let us find the derivative of the
exponential-power function y = x to the power x.

10
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:04.390
First we take the logarithm of both sides.
Here, treating y as a function of x,

11
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:10.500
we differentiate both sides. We get the
following equation.

12
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:17.200
We express y and cancel x. Substituting the
given function

13
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.540
for y, we get the answer in the following
form.

14
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.685
Suppose the dependence between an arbitrary
argument x and

15
00:01:26.685 --> 00:01:29.930
a function y is given by an equation not
solved for y.

16
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:35.950
Such a dependence defines y as an implicit
function of x.

17
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:42.740
Although this equation cannot be solved for y,
the derivative

18
00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.060
can be found by treating y as a function of x.

19
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.380
To find the derivative of y with respect to x,

20
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:55.400
we differentiate the equation in the variables
x and y,

21
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.237
treating y as a composite function of x,

22
00:01:58.237 --> 00:02:02.280
that is, we take the derivative and also
multiply by y′ₓ.

23
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:08.600
In the resulting expression we collect the
like terms containing y′ₓ,

24
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:16.820
and by solving this equation we find the
derivative y′ₓ.

25
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:28.080
Let us look at an example. Let the implicit
function x² + x·y² = 0 be given.

26
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.609
Let us find its derivative. Keeping in mind
that

27
00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:34.770
y depends on x and is a composite function,

28
00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.940
we differentiate both sides of the equation.

29
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.470
We take the left side as the derivative of a
sum.

30
00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:47.530
The second term is a product of two functions,
so

31
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:53.830
we compute it using the product rule. From the
resulting

32
00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:57.070
expression we keep y′ₓ on the left-hand side
of the equation,

33
00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:03.910
collect like terms, and from the resulting
equality find the derivative explicitly.

34
00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:12.670
Let a function y(x) be given through a
parameter t: x = x(t)

35
00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:18.930
and y = y(t) are functions depending on t.

36
00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:25.250
The derivative of a parametrically defined
function is y′ₓ equals y′ₜ

37
00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.950
divided by x′ₜ.

38
00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.220
Let us go through the formula with an example.

39
00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:39.770
Let a function be given in parametric form.
First we find the derivative x′ₜ,

40
00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:46.090
then we find y′ₜ. Substituting the functions
we found into the formula,

41
00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:52.660
we take the common factors outside the
brackets and then cancel the fraction

42
00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.700
by two. As a result, we get the following
fraction.

43
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.033
In this lesson we learned to find derivatives
of

44
00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:06.400
parametrically defined and implicit functions,

45
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:12.580
and we also looked at the method of
logarithmic differentiation.
